Under Article 51A of the Indian Constitution, the fundamental duties are as follows:

(51) Fundamental duties.—It shall be the duty of every citizen of India—

(a) to abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals and institutions, the National Flag and the National Anthem;

(b) to cherish and follow the noble ideals which inspired our national struggle for freedom; 

(c) to uphold and protect the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India;

(d) to defend the country and render national service when called upon to do so;

(e) to promote harmony and the spirit of common brotherhood among all the people of India transcending religious, linguistic and regional or sectional diversities; 

(f) to renounce practices derogatory to the dignity of women;

(g) to value and preserve the rich heritage of our composite culture;

(h) to protect and improve the natural environment including forests, lakes, rivers and wild life, and to have compassion for living creatures;

(i) to develop the scientific temper, humanism and the spirit of international peace and security; 

(j) to promote and support the principles of democracy, freedom, equality and justice in all its forms.

These fundamental duties are essential for maintaining the unity and integrity of India and protecting its sovereignty.

A person shall not be qualified to be chosen to fill a seat in Parliament unless he—

3. (a) is a citizen of India, and makes and subscribes before some person authorised in that behalf by the Election Commission an oath or affirmation according to the form set out for the purpose in the Third Schedule;  
(b) is, in the case of a seat in the Council of States, not less than thirty years of age and, in the case of a seat in the House of the People, not less than twenty-five years of age;  
(c) possesses such other qualifications as may be prescribed in that behalf by or under any law made by Parliament.

The provisions of clauses (1), (2) and (3) shall apply in relation to persons who by virtue of this Constitution have the right to speak in, and otherwise to take part in the proceedings of, a House of Parliament or any committee thereof as they apply in relation to members of Parliament.

The key features of Fundamental Duties under Article 51A include:
(a) Adherence to the Constitution and respect for its ideals and institutions;
(b) Cherishing and following the noble ideals that inspired our national struggle for freedom; 
(c) Upholding and protecting the sovereignty, unity, integrity, and national interest of India;
(d) Defending the country and rendering national service when called upon to do so;
(e) Promoting harmony and the spirit of common brotherhood among all people of India transcending religious, linguistic, and regional or sectional diversities; 
(f) Respecting women's rights;
(g) Valuing and preserving the rich heritage of our composite culture; 
(h) Protecting and improving the natural environment including forests, lakes, rivers, and wild life; and 
(i) Developing the scientific temper, humanism, and spirit of democracy.

Under the Consumer Protection Act, 2019 (hereinafter referred to as the "Act"), a product seller is liable in a product liability action if:

(a) The product contains a manufacturing defect;
(b) The product is defective in design; 
(c) There is a deviation from manufacturing specifications; 
(d) The product does not conform to the express warranty; or
(e) The product fails to contain adequate instructions of correct usage to prevent any harm or any warning regarding improper or incorrect usage.

Product liability actions can be brought by a complainant against a product manufacturer, product service provider, or product seller, as the case may be. In such an action, if any of these conditions are met, the product seller is liable for any harm caused to the complainant on account thereof.
The Act provides that the product manufacturer shall be liable in a product liability action if any of the above-mentioned defects exist.

Section 2 provides that "complaint" means any allegation in writing made by a complainant for obtaining any relief provided by or under this Act, that—

(i) an unfair contract or unfair trade practice or a restrictive trade practice has been adopted by any trader or service provider;
(ii) the goods bought by him or agreed to be bought by him suffer from one or more defects;
(iii) the services hired or availed of or agreed to be hired or availed of by him suffer from any deficiency;

and that—

(iv) a trader or a service provider, as the case may be, has charged for the goods or for the services mentioned in the complaint, a price in excess of the price—
(a) fixed by or under any law for the time being in force; or

Definitions.

Section 2 addresses the definition of a complaint in the following manner:

(1) "Complaint" means any allegation in writing, made by a complainant for obtaining any relief provided by or under this Act, that—

(i) an unfair contract or unfair trade practice or a restrictive trade practice has been adopted by any trader or service provider;

(ii) the goods bought by him or agreed to be bought by him suffer from one or more defects; 

(iii) the services hired or availed of or agreed to be hired or availed of by him suffer from any deficiency;

(iv) a trader or a service provider, as the case may be, has charged for the goods or for the services mentioned in the complaint, a price in excess of the price—
(a) fixed by or under any law for the time being in force; or

Definitions.

The definition is provided to specify what constitutes an allegation in writing that alleges unfair contract or trade practice.

