Definitions.

Under the Consumer Protection Act, 2019 (Consumer Protection Act), Chapter IX, Section 47, "unfair trade practice" is defined as a trade practice that adopts any unfair method or unfair or deceptive practice including but not limited to:

(i) Making any statement, whether orally or in writing or by visible representation including by means of electronic record, which—
   (a) falsely represents that the goods are of a particular standard, quality, quantity, grade, composition, style or model;
   (b) falsely represents that the services are of a particular standard, quality or grade;
   (c) falsely represents any re-built, second-hand, renovated, reconditioned or old goods as new goods;

(ii) Represents that the goods or services have sponsorship, approval, performance, characteristics, accessories, uses or benefits which such person has not in fact.

Under the Consumer Protection Act, 2019, an unfair trade practice is defined as a trade practice that adopts any unfair method or unfair or deceptive practice. These practices include:

(i) Making any statement, whether orally or in writing, which falsely represents that goods are of a particular standard, quality, quantity, grade, composition, style, or model;
(ii) Making any statement, whether orally or in writing, which falsely represents that services are of a particular standard, quality, or grade;
(iii) Making any statement, whether orally or in writing, which falsely represents any re-built, second-hand, renovated, reconditioned, or old goods as new goods; 
(iv) Representing that the goods or services have sponsorship, approval, performance, characteristics, accessories, uses, or benefits which such

(v) Charging a price in excess of what is fixed by or under any law for the time being in force for the sale of goods or services, or for the provision of any service; 
(vi) Adhering to terms which are unfair and deceptive practices that are prejudicial to the public interest or to the interests of consumers;
(vii) Adhering to terms which are unfair and deceptive practices that are detrimental to the interests of consumers.

The Act also provides a detailed framework for consumer protection, including provisions on complaints, investigations, guidelines for preventing unfair trade practices, and safety notices.

(45) "trader", in relation to any goods, means a person who sells or distributes 
any goods for sale and includes the manufacturer thereof, and where such goods are 
sold or distributed in package form, includes the packer thereof;
